1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,008
How does the bank know a new merchant really owns the email they typed?

2
00:00:04,058 --> 00:00:08,616
Anyone can type any address, so a sign-up alone proves nothing.

3
00:00:08,666 --> 00:00:14,688
The contract adds three calls: register, verify the email, and resend the code.

4
00:00:14,738 --> 00:00:19,968
Registering saves the merchant as pending, and refuses an email that is already taken.

5
00:00:20,018 --> 00:00:26,088
A one-time code then goes to that email, from the OTP library that any module can use.

6
00:00:26,138 --> 00:00:34,200
Redis keeps only keyed hashes of the code and the email, and one script checks every limit and stores the code in a single step.

7
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:39,552
The email goes out through the notification service, and locally it lands in Mailpit.

8
00:00:39,602 --> 00:00:45,000
The merchant sends the code back, and only then is the email marked as verified.

9
00:00:45,050 --> 00:00:52,080
Wrong codes count across the whole send window, so a lockout lasts until the window ends, even for a new code.

10
00:00:52,130 --> 00:00:59,976
Sends are limited per window, and a new code waits a configured pause after the last one, so nobody can flood an inbox.

11
00:01:00,026 --> 00:01:05,328
Every refusal that lifts by itself carries a Retry-After header that says when.

12
00:01:05,378 --> 00:01:11,616
Without Redis, no code is sent or accepted: the library fails closed with a five oh three.

13
00:01:11,666 --> 00:01:22,704
Every failure has its own stable code, and its message in English, Russian and Uzbek: email taken, code invalid, code expired, too many attempts, or too early.

14
00:01:22,754 --> 00:01:28,536
The limits are the library's own defaults, and the app supplies only the secret, from Vault.

15
00:01:28,586 --> 00:01:33,240
Next, the merchant moves through a lifecycle, from pending to active.
